HARDWARE – ARDUINO-BASED REMOTE-CONTROLLED AND
OBSTACLE-AVOIDANCE WHEELED ROBOT
================================================

OVERVIEW
--------
This directory contains the hardware design, assembly, and prototype
evidence for the Arduino-based Remote-Controlled and Obstacle-Avoidance
Wheeled Robot.

The robot was developed as a wheeled mobile robotic platform integrating
Arduino-based control, DC geared motors, an L293D motor driver, IR remote
control, an ultrasonic sensor, and a servo motor for obstacle detection
and avoidance.


MAIN HARDWARE COMPONENTS
------------------------
- Arduino microcontroller
- Two DC geared motors
- L293D motor driver
- IR receiver module
- IR remote controller
- Ultrasonic distance sensor
- Servo motor
- Robot chassis/body
- Wheels
- Caster/dummy wheel
- Battery/power supply
- Connecting wires and supporting components


ROBOT CONTROL SYSTEM
--------------------
The Arduino serves as the main controller of the robot.

For remote-controlled operation, commands received from the IR remote
control are processed by the Arduino and converted into appropriate
motor-control signals through the L293D motor driver.

The two DC geared motors provide differential movement, including:

- Forward movement
- Reverse movement
- Left turn
- Right turn
- Stop


OBSTACLE-AVOIDANCE SYSTEM
-------------------------
The obstacle-avoidance section uses an ultrasonic distance sensor to
detect objects in the robot's path.

A servo motor is used to position the ultrasonic sensor and support
directional obstacle detection. Based on the detected distance, the
Arduino determines an appropriate movement response and controls the
DC geared motors accordingly.


MOTOR DRIVE
-----------
The L293D motor driver is used as the interface between the Arduino
controller and the DC geared motors.

It provides the required motor-control interface and allows the Arduino
to control the direction and operation of the two geared motors.


HARDWARE DEVELOPMENT PROCESS
----------------------------
The hardware development involved:

1. Selection of the robot control and sensing components.
2. Design of the electronic control circuit.
3. Simulation and verification of the circuit using Proteus.
4. Assembly of the motor driver and control circuitry.
5. Mechanical assembly of the robot chassis and wheels.
6. Installation of the ultrasonic sensor and servo motor.
7. Integration of the IR receiver and remote-control system.
8. Connection of the Arduino to the motor driver and sensors.
9. Programming and hardware-software integration.
10. Testing and verification of the completed robot prototype.


